enchilada: display: the scanout pool says who holds its slots, and why a frame missed it

Boot 27 (sweetzc=1, GL armed at 354 s): the CPU-mode surfaces 2 and 3 gave their slots back
when backboardd exited (GL surface 19 took 0x9eab4000 at 360.31, surface 20 0x9dd68000 at
360.73), but from surface 21 on no canvas surface got a slot again until the log ends at 497 s
(no further "in the scanout pool" line although 0251 logged the first 8 and only 4 were used,
and no "freed while ... still mapped"): surfaces 19 and 20 were never freed, while CA made a new
display surface every frame. Every later swap was copied ("not in the scanout pool (pool full,
or not a canvas-sized BGRA surface)"), and nothing said who kept 19 and 20.
The pool now records which surface has each slot (under a lock; give() clears the entry before
the surface's memory goes) and, rate-limited (the first 48 decisions, then one in 64; boot-arg
sweetzc_log=0: off), says:
  "SWEET SURF: surface N (1125x2436) in the scanout pool at PA ..."           (take)
  "SWEET FB: a new 1125x2436 surface of backboardd (#K): every scanout slot is taken: slot 0
   surface A (refs R, gpu G, on the panel|flip pending|not shown, T ms); ..."  (refused)
  "SWEET FB: surface N gives scanout slot I back after T ms (#K)"             (give)
refs is the surface's retain count: 1 for the id table, 1 per client mapping it, 1 per GPU
import (gpu, counted in SURFACE_BO / releaseGem), 1 for the panel and 1 for a pending flip, so
refs - 1 - gpu - panel/pending is the number of processes still holding it (CoreAnimation, or
eagl.c's image table). A slot freed while still mapped is marked leaked (logged always, as
before) instead of keeping a pointer to the freed surface. zcFlip's reason now tells "every slot
was taken when it was made" from "not a canvas-sized BGRA surface"; the 10 s line says
"pool slots in use B/N". No behaviour change besides the lines.

